==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{term|douagere|lang=frm}}, {{term|douagiere|lang=frm}}, from {{term|douage||dower|lang=frm}}, from the verb {{term|douer||to endow|lang=frm}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|dōtāre||to endow}}, from {{m|la|dōs}}, ''dōtis'' ("dowry").

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[widow]] holding [[property]] or [[title]] [[derived]] from her late [[husband]].
#* {{RQ:Chmbrs YngrSt|VI}}
#*:“I don't mean all of your friends—only a small proportion—which, however, connects your circle with that deadly, idle, brainless bunch—the insolent chatterers at the opera, the gorged '''dowagers''', the worn-out, passionless men, the enervated matrons of the summer capital, the chlorotic squatters on huge yachts,{{nb...}}!”
# Any [[lady]] of [[dignified]] [[bearing]].

